<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  keychain-access-groups was here, declaring:

	      $(AppIdentifierPrefix)com.fasahahaus.inventoryAppPos

	  while the bundle identifier in Runner.xcodeproj is

	      com.fasahahaus-co.inventoryAppPos

	  Both were written in the same commit (2f528a5, Feb 2026), so this was a typo at
	  birth rather than drift — the "-co" is missing.

	  The consequence is not cosmetic. flutter_secure_storage only sets
	  kSecAttrAccessGroup when a groupId is passed in IOSOptions, and this app passes
	  none (lib/data/local_storage_service_impl.dart), so every keychain item goes to
	  the first group listed here. A group that is neither the app's own
	  application-identifier nor a registered Keychain Sharing group is refused — the
	  project has no Keychain Sharing capability — and the calls come back
	  errSecMissingEntitlement.

	  Which would be survivable if it were loud. It is not:
	  _getOrGenerateEncryptionKey catches PlatformException and returns null, so Hive
	  opens its boxes unencrypted and the app starts perfectly happily with the session
	  and the offline sale queue unreadable. The same silent failure being guarded
	  against on Windows, on a platform where nobody is watching for it yet.

	  Removed rather than corrected to the right string. An app that shares its keychain
	  with nothing — no extensions, no sibling apps, no groupId anywhere in the code —
	  does not need this entitlement at all. Without it iOS defaults to the app's own
	  application-identifier, which means the group follows the bundle identifier
	  automatically and can never disagree with it again. Writing the identifier out by
	  hand would just reinstate the thing that broke: two places holding the same string.

	  If keychain sharing is ever genuinely needed (an app extension, a second app
	  reading the same items), add the capability in Xcode and put the group back here —
	  the file and its CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS wiring are left in place for that.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
